{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WVQADYtq6uF38KepzH92L4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose tasks for release 03.0.\n\n## Step 1: Analyze Project Context\n\nThe project is `go-unix-utils` — a specification-driven regeneration of Unix utilities in Go. The pipeline is: extract specs from C source → specify (PRD + use case) → synthesize (AI generates Go code) → verify (differential testing).\n\n## Step 2: Summarize Project State\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge in observable behavior.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` — one package per utility (main.go + tests)\n- `pkg/sys/` — syscall abstractions (Darwin/Linux)\n\n\n- `pkg/format/` — output formatting\n- `pkg/testutils/` — differential testing harness\n- `magefiles/` — build targets\n- `docs/` — specs, PRDs, use cases, test suites\n\n**Current state**: Several utilities have completed specs and code at generation-merged tags (ts, wc, cat, sponge, pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format), but the working branch has no generated code. Earlier releases marked specs as \"implemented\" while code readiness shows \"none\" and code status shows \"not\" started.\n\n**Current focus**: I'm working on release 03.0 only, which covers the `wc` (word count) utility. The spec is complete but implementation hasn't begun yet. I need to find the PRD for wc and start building the `cmd/wc` command in Go.\n\nLooking at the status more carefully, there's already a PRD for wc from a previous release (rel01.1), and code was generated at some point but lives only in the generation-merged tags, not on the working branch. So I need to generate the wc implementation fresh.\n\nSince I'm limited to one task and the constraint requires splitting implementation from tests, I need to decide which one to tackle first. I'm seeing the implementation status details now — wc, cat, and sponge are all spec complete at rel01.1, and the code_status shows wc was part of batch 3 with differential testing. Since I can't directly access the filesystem, I'm trying to infer the PRD number by looking at the sequential naming pattern, but without being able to read the actual files, I'll need to make a reasonable reference based on what PRDs I know exist. I'm mapping out the file paths for the test suite and use cases—test-rel-03.0.yaml for the release tests and rel03.0-uc001-wc.yaml for the wc use case. For the actual implementation task, I need to focus on cmd/wc/main.go with the core flags like -l, -w, -c, -m, and -L, keeping it under 250 lines while covering at most 4 PRD requirements.\n\nThe challenge is that I don't have the PRD content inline to cite specific requirement IDs, but the project context references rel03.0-uc001-wc as the use case, so I'll anchor the task to that and let the stitch agent pull the actual PRD requirements from the spec file. The required_reading field serves this purpose — the stitch agent will read the PRD.\n\nBut I still need to reference specific requirement IDs in the task. Without knowing them, I need to make reasonable assumptions based on what wc does.\n\nActually, re-reading the constraints more carefully: \"Do NOT explore the filesystem, read files, or run commands. All project information is in the project_context above.\"\n\nThe project_context doesn't contain the actual PRD content for wc. So I need to work with what I have and reference the PRD file for the stitch agent to read.\n\nLooking at the example, it references the PRD by filename but uses generic requirement IDs (R1, R2, R3) within the task itself — so the task requirements have their own numbering separate from the PRD. Since I don't have the actual PRD content, I should reference the PRD path and create task requirements based on what I can infer from the project context. I need to figure out the correct PRD file path for wc, but without knowing the exact numbering scheme, I'll have to make an educated guess based on the pattern of implementations and the orphaned PRDs I've seen. Since there are roughly nine tools before the orphaned prd012 and prd013, the wc PRD is likely somewhere in that range, though I can't pin down the exact number without more information. I'm working through the naming pattern—prd011 is for magefiles based on the code generation reference, so wc would likely be somewhere in the prd001-prd010 range as part of the initial batch. Rather than guess the exact number, I'll use the glob pattern `docs/specs/product-requirements/prd*-wc.yaml` and let the system resolve it, since I have the test suite and use case paths that follow the same structure. Rather than speculate on the exact PRD numbering, I'll reference the pattern so the stitch agent can locate it. For the actual task, I'm proposing the implementation of cmd/wc for Release 03.0 Batch 3, which needs to handle line, word, byte, and character counting with support for flags like -l, -w, -c, -m, and -L across multiple files.\n\nNow I'm realizing there's a dependency issue — the foundation packages in release 00.0 haven't been started yet, but I'm constrained to only propose tasks for release 03.0. I need to check whether those earlier releases are actually prerequisites or if I can work around them.\n\nLooking at the constraint more carefully, I should propose the wc task for release 03.0 and trust that the stitch agent will handle any dependencies when it reads the PRD. I should also keep in mind that the PRD might have more than the allowed 4 sub-requirements, so I'll need to be selective about what goes into the task definition. I'm realizing I need to anchor to specific PRD requirement IDs, but without the actual PRD content available, I'll need to reference it in the required_reading section and let the stitch agent map the task requirements to the PRD. Let me draft a task that describes the core functionality to implement while keeping it generic enough to work with whatever the PRD specifies. I'm scoping out the core wc functionality—counting lines, words, bytes, characters, and max line length with proper flag handling, stdin support, and formatted output—which should fit in a single task at around 150-250 lines of implementation code. Now I need to figure out the file structure and whether there are any package dependencies to consider. I'm filling in the remaining requirements—handling all the command-line flags, stdin with file arguments and totals, plus proper exit codes and error handling as specified in the PRD. The design follows the existing cmd/ package structure with GNU wc-compatible output formatting and LC_ALL=C for test consistency, and I need to verify the acceptance criteria around compilation, linting, and correct output across different input scenarios.\n\nSince I don't have the exact PRD filename, I'm considering whether to guess the number, use a descriptive reference, or point to the specs directory and let the agent locate it based on the implementation phases and orphaned PRD patterns. I'm mapping out the likely PRD assignments based on the release versions and creation order, though without access to the filesystem I can't confirm the exact numbering for utilities like wc. Rather than guess, I should reference the path pattern so the agent can locate the correct required_reading files dynamically. I'm realizing the stitch agent needs explicit file paths to work with, so I should specify the exact naming pattern for the PRD file rather than leaving it vague, and include the architecture document since it covers the cmd component structure. Let me finalize the required_reading list with clear, discoverable paths. So the spec is complete for wc in release 03.0. I'm thinking through the dependencies—the foundational packages from earlier releases might not be available, but wc is straightforward enough that I can implement it using just the standard library: stdin and file I/O with bufio for reading, and fmt for output.\n\nI also need the flag package for parsing command-line arguments, so the whole implementation can live in cmd/wc/main.go without external dependencies.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the architecture—pkg/testutils would be needed for the test suite, but since I'm only handling the implementation here, that's separate. I'm also clarifying the constraint about task requirements: it's limited to 4 PRD sub-requirements, not top-level ones, so I can structure the task around the core wc functionality areas. I need to implement the core wc utility with flag parsing for all the standard options like line, word, byte, and character counts, handle both stdin and file inputs with proper totals, and ensure proper exit codes and error handling as specified in the PRD.\n\nThe output formatting needs to match GNU wc exactly with right-aligned columns and the same field ordering, and I'll stick to Go's standard library without any external dependencies. I also need to follow the cmd/ package structure with main.go as the entry point, make sure the code compiles and passes vet, and verify that all flags work correctly across single files, multiple files, and stdin input.\n\nNow I'm checking the acceptance criteria count against the requirements -- I have 6 criteria but only 4 requirements listed, and the constraints say at most 4 PRD sub-requirements per task. I need to clarify whether the distinction is between PRD sub-requirements (specific IDs from the PRD) and overall task requirements, since the guidelines call for 5-8 requirements total but cap PRD sub-requirements at 4. There's a tension here between P9's guidance on 5-8 requirements for code tasks and this prompt's constraint limiting me to at most 4 PRD sub-requirements per task. The specific constraint takes precedence, so I'll cap requirements at 4. For acceptance criteria, I'll aim for 5-6 to align with P9's range, and my 3 design decisions already fit within the 3-5 target.\n\nThe bigger issue is anchoring tasks to specific PRD requirement IDs without having the actual PRD content. The constraint asks for exact IDs like \"prd001 R1.1\", but I can only reference the PRD file generically and describe functional areas—the stitch agent will need to map those to actual requirements when implementing. I'm laying out the core requirements for the wc implementation — handling the standard flags for line, word, byte, and character counting with proper defaults, then moving into the specific behavioral requirements that the PRD and test suite define. I need to match GNU wc's output format exactly with right-aligned numeric columns and space-separated fields, then the filename. I'm also constrained to only Go's standard library for this implementation, and I need to ensure the binary compiles cleanly, passes vet checks, and handles all the flags from the PRD.\n\nNow I'm verifying the acceptance criteria are complete—the implementation needs to handle stdin with no arguments, accept all defined flags, process multiple files with per-file and total counts, and properly report missing files to stderr while continuing to process remaining arguments.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19048,"cache_read_input_tokens":16066,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":19048,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0802a7c2-02a8-429e-a382-c8f5b3a8d7d0","uuid":"713b14af-0334-43bb-8e8a-7c7d53fe667d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WVQADYtq6uF38KepzH92L4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge in observable behavior because man pages omit edge cases, flag interactions, and exit-code contracts.\n\n**Architecture**: A four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) that derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations via mage-claude-orchestrator, and verifies parity through differential testing against Homebrew GNU reference binaries. Key components: `cmd/` (one package per utility), `pkg/sys/` (syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `magefiles/` (build targets).\n\n**Current state**: Specifications are complete for several utilities (ts, wc, cat, sponge, ls, du) and shared packages (testutils, sys, format). Generated code exists at `generation-merged` tags but not on the working branch. No code has been generated for the current release cycle.\n\n**Current release**: Release 03.0 — \"Batch 3 — wc specification and differential test\". Use case `rel03.0-uc001-wc` has spec status `spec_complete` and code status `not started`.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nRelease 03.0 has a single use case: `rel03.0-uc001-wc`. The wc specification is complete (PRD, use case, test suite all exist). The next step is implementation. Since implementation and tests must be split into separate tasks, and I'm limited to 1 task, I propose the implementation task first. The wc utility (line/word/byte counting) is simple enough to fit within 150–250 lines in a single `main.go`.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement cmd/wc counting and output formatting -- prd-wc core\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/ (find the wc PRD file, named prdNNN-wc.yaml -- read all its requirements)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel03.0-uc001-wc.yaml (use case for wc)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-03.0.yaml (test suite -- understand expected inputs and outputs)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (cmd/ component pattern and design decisions DD1 through DD6)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/wc/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"wc utility -- flag parsing, counting engine, output formatting, error handling\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement line (-l), word (-w), byte (-c), and character (-m) counting as specified in the wc PRD. When no flags are given, default to lines, words, and bytes.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement maximum display width (-L) as specified in the wc PRD. Count bytes per line and report the maximum across all lines.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Read from stdin when no file arguments are provided. When one or more file paths are given, count each file and print per-file results. When more than one file is given, append a total summary line.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Set exit code to 0 on success. On file-open errors, print a diagnostic to stderr in the format used by GNU wc and set exit code to 1, but continue processing remaining files.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the cmd/ one-package-per-utility pattern from ARCHITECTURE.yaml. The package is cmd/wc/ with a single main.go file.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Match GNU wc output format exactly -- right-aligned numeric columns with dynamic width, fields separated by single spaces, filename printed after counts. This is required for differential testing parity (ARCHITECTURE DD2).\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Use only Go standard library (os, fmt, bufio, flag, io, unicode/utf8). cmd/ packages do not import external dependencies (ARCHITECTURE technology choices). Do not import pkg/sys or pkg/format.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/wc/main.go exists and compiles with 'go build ./cmd/wc/'\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"'go vet ./cmd/wc/' passes with no diagnostics\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Binary counts lines, words, and bytes from stdin when invoked with no arguments and no flags\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"All flags defined in the wc PRD (-l, -w, -c, -m, -L) are accepted and produce correct output\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"Multiple file arguments produce per-file counts followed by a total line\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"A missing file argument produces a stderr diagnostic and exit code 1 while remaining files are still counted and printed\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19048,"cache_read_input_tokens":16066,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":19048,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0802a7c2-02a8-429e-a382-c8f5b3a8d7d0","uuid":"b77c3e13-bcd4-493d-b8df-4009f3fdd2e1"}
